@database "llm_anthropic"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_anthropic.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Anthropic LLM provider"
@{h1}Anthropic LLM provider

This plugin lets agents talk to Claude through Anthropic's own Messages API,
using the official @{tt}anthropic@{utt} SDK, and runs Claude requests through
the Message Batches API. It is an LLM provider plugin: it has no tools, no
hooks and no panel. You use it by writing @{tt}provider: anthropic@{utt} in a
model entry of @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}; agents reach that entry through a
profile.

@{table}
Provider name | Kind | Endpoint
anthropic | chat | POST <base_url>/v1/messages, always streamed
anthropic | batch | the Message Batches API at api.anthropic.com
@{body}

Claude behind OpenRouter is not this plugin: that goes through
@{tt}openai_httpx@{utt} (@{" OpenAI-compatible providers " link llm_openai_compat/main}).
How models, profiles and fallback chains work in general is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}; this manual covers what
this provider does with an entry.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  Timeouts and streaming  " link timeouts}
  @{"  Retries  " link retries}
  @{"  Thinking  " link thinking}
  @{"  Prompt caching  " link caching}
  @{"  Usage, cost and the hooks  " link usage}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}
  @{"  Batch  " link batch}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

The keys of a model entry this provider reads. Everything else in
@{tt}LLMModelConfig@{utt} is either handled by the core (@{tt}extends@{utt},
profiles) or not read here.

@{table}
Key | Default | What the client does with it
provider | -- | anthropic
model | -- | the model id sent to the API
api_key | ANTHROPIC_API_KEY | without either, building the client fails ("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is required when provider=anthropic")
base_url | the SDK's: ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, else https://api.anthropic.com | the endpoint, without /v1
context_window | 200000 | only reported to the core (compaction, context sizing), never sent
request_timeout | 180 | seconds; see @{" Timeouts and streaming " link timeouts}
max_tokens | 8192 | output cap, always sent; 0 also means 8192
temperature | none | sent when set -- except on tool calls while thinking is on
include_thoughts | false | turns thinking on for tool calls (see @{" Thinking " link thinking})
thinking_request_shape | budget | budget or adaptive: the form of the thinking request
thinking_budget | 8192 | budget_tokens, only with thinking_request_shape: budget
reasoning_details_mode | keep_all | how many turns' thinking blocks travel back
enable_prompt_caching | true | cache markers on the system prompt, the last tool and (by mode) the conversation
prompt_cache_mode | none | whether the conversation's tail gets a marker too (see @{" Prompt caching " link caching})
capabilities | all defaults | streaming decides which call the agent makes; image_input gates images a tool returns; structured_output allows response formats; developer_role cannot go above user
batch_provider | none | anthropic sends this entry's batch requests here (see @{" Batch " link batch})
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}thinking_level@{utt} is not wired: the client logs "thinking_level
is not wired for provider=anthropic and will be ignored" when it is built. Use
include_thoughts and the request shape.
@{bullet}@{tt}tool_schema_dialect@{utt}, @{tt}assistant_reasoning_field@{utt},
@{tt}stream_silence_timeout@{utt} and @{tt}provider_affinity_minutes@{utt} are
logged as "not wired ... will be ignored". Other keys --
@{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt}, the prompt_cache_key/marker keys, OpenRouter
routing, safety settings, service tier -- are not read at all.
@{bullet}An unknown value for thinking_request_shape or reasoning_details_mode
is refused when the client is built.

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    claude-opus-5-thinking:
      provider: anthropic
      model: claude-opus-5
      api_key: ${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}
      thinking_request_shape: adaptive
      include_thoughts: true
      context_window: 1000000
      request_timeout: 600
      max_tokens: 32768
@{body}
@endnode

@node timeouts "Timeouts and streaming"
@{h1}Timeouts and streaming

@{h2}Streaming or not

The client always streams on the wire for the agent's calls: with
@{tt}capabilities.streaming: false@{utt} the agent server waits for the whole
answer instead of showing it as it arrives, but the request underneath is the
same stream. Only @{tt}chat()@{utt} -- a plain text call without tools, which
the @{" LLM router " link llm_router/main} and other helpers use -- sends one
non-streamed request.

@{h2}The timeout

@{tt}request_timeout@{utt} (default 180 s) is handed to the SDK as one number,
so it is the connect, read, write and pool timeout at once. While streaming,
read means the longest gap between two pieces of the stream. The API sends
keep-alive pings during long pauses, which reset it; a model thinking for
minutes without visible output is therefore not cut, but neither is an
upstream that only pings. @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} and the system's
@{tt}llm_system.httpx_timeouts@{utt} are not read by this provider.

@{h2}Cancelling

A cancelled run ends the stream at once, also while nothing but pings
arrives -- the pause of a model thinking with its text omitted is not billed
to the end. The cancellation reaches the agent as a cancellation, never as an
answer. @{tt}chat()@{utt} is watched the same way while its request is out.

@{h2}How a stream ends

An answer is handed over when the SDK has assembled the final message: its
text, tool calls, thinking blocks with their signatures, usage and stop
reason. A stream that breaks off before (dropped connection, read timeout,
an error event from the API) is retried from scratch, see
@{" Retries " link retries}.
@endnode

@node retries "Retries"
@{h1}Retries

The SDK's own retries are off; the client retries itself. Every retry is
reported to the post_llm_response hooks as its own row (@{tt}[RETRY n/m]
...@{utt}, finish reason @{tt}retry@{utt}) and as a status line in the chat.
A cancelled run interrupts every wait. A retry starts from scratch: when the
caller has already received deltas of the failed attempt, the stream first
sends one @{tt}{"type": "stream_restart"}@{utt} chunk, and the caller drops
what it has shown of that attempt.

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
HTTP 429, or a rate_limit_error event in the stream | up to 2 retries; wait: the retry-after header's seconds, else 60 s, plus 0-50 % jitter (a value that is no wait -- negative, inf, nan -- counts as none). A retry-after above 60 s is not slept: LLMRateLimitError at once with that retry_after, so the server blocks the model and falls back. After the retries: LLMQuotaExhaustedError if the message says "quota" or "exhausted", else LLMRateLimitError
HTTP 5xx (529 overloaded included), or an overloaded_error / api_error event in the stream | up to 3 retries after 2 s, 4 s, 8 s, then LLMServerError
connection error, read timeout, a stream that drops | up to 3 retries after 2 s, 4 s, 8 s, then LLMConnectionError
any other 4xx | no retry: httpx.HTTPStatusError
@{body}

All retries share one attempt counter: at most four attempts in all, and a
429 is retried only while fewer than three attempts have been made. The counts
are fixed by the provider (max_retries 3, rate limit 2); no entry key changes
them.

@{tt}chat()@{utt} makes a single attempt; its failures are typed the same way.

The agent server's own fallback chain (@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} with more than
one profile) takes over where these retries end; see
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}.
@endnode

@node thinking "Thinking"
@{h1}Thinking

@{h2}Asking for it

@{tt}include_thoughts: true@{utt} adds a thinking request to every tool call
(the agent's steps). @{tt}chat()@{utt} never asks for thinking. The form is
declared per entry, never guessed from the model name:

@{table}
thinking_request_shape | Sent | For
budget (default) | thinking: {type: enabled, budget_tokens: <thinking_budget or 8192>} | models that take a fixed budget (Haiku 4.5)
adaptive | thinking: {type: adaptive} | models that decide themselves and refuse a budget with 400 (Opus 5); thinking_budget is ignored
@{body}

With the budget form the API requires the budget to be below
@{tt}max_tokens@{utt}. Both default to 8192, so an entry that turns thinking
on must set @{tt}max_tokens@{utt} (or a smaller @{tt}thinking_budget@{utt}):
an entry whose budget is not below its max_tokens is refused when the client
is built, naming the model and both values -- not by a 400 on every call.

While thinking is on, @{tt}temperature@{utt} is left out of tool calls: the
API refuses sampling settings together with thinking.

@{h2}Watching it

Thinking the model shows arrives in the chat live as thinking deltas. Newer
models (Opus 5, Sonnet 5) omit the text by default: then nothing is shown,
while the thinking still happens and is billed.

@{h2}Sending it back

The final message's thinking and redacted_thinking blocks, signatures
included, are stored verbatim on the assistant message together with the
model that produced them. They travel back in the order the model emitted
them, before the text and the tool calls: the API refuses a tool round trip
whose thinking blocks are missing or changed.

@{bullet}@{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt}: @{tt}keep_all@{utt} (default)
sends every turn's blocks back and keeps the cached prefix byte-identical;
@{tt}keep_last@{utt} only the latest assistant message's; @{tt}strip@{utt}
none.
@{bullet}Blocks from another model (a fallback profile answered that turn)
are dropped: the API would ignore them but bill them as input.
@{bullet}A plain text turn keeps its blocks only beside non-empty text.

@{h2}Usage

Anthropic counts thinking in @{tt}output_tokens@{utt} and reports no separate
reasoning figure, so the usage has no
@{tt}completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens@{utt}.
@endnode

@node caching "Prompt caching"
@{h1}Prompt caching

How the core splits a prompt into cacheable blocks is described in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}. This provider places
Anthropic's @{tt}cache_control@{utt} markers itself, with
@{tt}enable_prompt_caching@{utt} on (the default):

@{bullet}on the last block of the system prompt, always;
@{bullet}on the last tool definition, always;
@{bullet}on the conversation's tail, depending on @{tt}prompt_cache_mode@{utt}:

@{table}
prompt_cache_mode | Tail marker
multi_turn | from the first call on
auto, or unset | once the conversation has history (an assistant or tool message)
task_sequence, one_shot, off | never
@{body}

Never more than Anthropic's four markers go out; if there were more, the
oldest are removed.

@{b}Turning cache writes off.@{ub} @{tt}prompt_cache_mode: off@{utt} only
drops the tail marker -- the system prompt and the tools are still written to
the cache. Only @{tt}enable_prompt_caching: false@{utt} sends no marker at
all. The markers sit on the static prefix, so a second call of the same agent
within the cache lifetime already reads it at a tenth of the price; turning
caching off saves only the write surcharge, and only where the prefix never
recurs.

@{h2}Keeping the prefix stable

@{bullet}A developer note is never given a marker: its text changes every
call. Notes are turned into user turns only after the markers are placed.
@{bullet}With @{tt}keep_all@{utt}, older thinking blocks stay in the history
unchanged turn by turn.
@{bullet}A response format goes out on every step of a run: changing it
invalidates the conversation's cache.
@endnode

@node usage "Usage, cost and the hooks"
@{h1}Usage, cost and the hooks

Each request is reported to the @{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt} hooks before it
is sent, and to the @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt} hooks when it ends --
@{tt}chat()@{utt} as well as the agent's tool calls. The message debugger,
the OpenTelemetry chat span and the cost figures are built from these reports
(@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks},
@{" The capture hooks " link message_debugger/hooks},
@{" What gets exported " link otel/spans},
@{" What it records " link context_usage_tracker/hooks}).

@{h2}What the hooks see

The pre_llm_request report carries the request as sent to the SDK: model,
messages, system, tools, max_tokens, thinking, output_config -- with its
cache markers. Provider @{tt}anthropic@{utt}, the endpoint as url,
@{tt}is_streaming@{utt} true for tool calls and false for @{tt}chat()@{utt}.

@{h2}One report per ending

@{table}
The request ended with | post_llm_response carries
an answer | usage, finish reason
a retry | an error @{tt}[RETRY n/m] ...@{utt}, finish reason retry, no usage
a refusal after the retries | the error (@{tt}HTTP 400: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Rate limit: ...@{utt}, @{tt}HTTP 529: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Network/protocol error: ...@{utt}), no usage
a cancellation while the request was out | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during streaming@{utt} (or @{tt}cancelled@{utt} when the run's task itself was cancelled)
a cancellation during a retry wait | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during retry wait@{utt}, after the retry's row
a cancellation before it was sent | the error @{tt}Request cancelled by user@{utt}
a stream the caller stopped reading | the error @{tt}stream abandoned by the caller@{utt}
anything unexpected | the exception's text
@{body}

A cancelled stream is billed for what the model generated, but its usage
comes with the final message, so the report has none: the tokens of a
cancelled call are missing from the figures.

@{h2}The usage

Mapped to the OpenAI shape every consumer reads. Anthropic's
@{tt}input_tokens@{utt} counts only the input that was neither read from nor
written to the cache, so:

@{table}
Field | Value
prompt_tokens | input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens
completion_tokens | output_tokens (thinking included)
total_tokens | the sum of both
prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens | cache_read_input_tokens
prompt_tokens_details.cache_creation_tokens | cache_creation_input_tokens
@{body}

The details are there only when the cache was read or written. The core
prices the tokens from @{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt}; the API reports no
cost of its own.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

What reaches the agent server when a request cannot be answered. The server
decides from the type whether to fall back to the next profile of the chain
(@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}); none of the SDK's own
exceptions is such a type, so the client translates them.

@{table}
Raised | When
LLMRateLimitError | 429 after the retries, with a retry_after
LLMQuotaExhaustedError | the same, with "quota" or "exhausted" in the message. Unverified: an exhausted credit balance is said to come as a 400 invalid_request_error ("Your credit balance is too low"), which is raised as httpx.HTTPStatusError, not as this; no such answer has been seen here
LLMServerError | 5xx or an overload/api error event after the retries; its status is the HTTP status, 529 for an overload event inside a stream
httpx.HTTPStatusError | any other 4xx; the message is @{tt}HTTP <status>: <the API's message>@{utt}. 401 to 404 make the server block this model for every agent for a while
LLMConnectionError | connection error, timeout or dropped stream after the retries
asyncio.CancelledError | the run was cancelled
StructuredOutputUnsupported | a response format this entry cannot send, refused before the request
ValueError at build time | no API key; an unknown thinking_request_shape or reasoning_details_mode; thinking with the budget form whose budget is not below max_tokens
@{body}

An error event of another kind inside a stream (an invalid request found
mid-way) is raised as httpx.HTTPStatusError with the stream's status 200.

@{h2}Stop reasons

@{table}
stop_reason | finish reason the agent sees
end_turn, stop_sequence | stop
tool_use | tool_calls
max_tokens, model_context_window_exceeded | length
refusal | content_filter
anything else | passed through as it is
@{body}
@endnode

@node batch "Batch"
@{h1}Batch

A model entry with @{tt}provider: batch@{utt} and @{tt}batch_provider:
anthropic@{utt} runs its requests
through Anthropic's Message Batches API: all requests of a job in one
@{tt}POST /v1/messages/batches@{utt}, polled until it has ended, the results
downloaded when it is done. The queue, its polling and its settings
(@{tt}llm_system.batch.providers.anthropic@{utt}) belong to the core; see
@{" Batch Monitor " link batch_monitor/main}.

@{bullet}Always api.anthropic.com, with the entry's @{tt}api_key@{utt} or
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY; without a key the backend is skipped (the core logs it).
base_url is not used.
@{bullet}Each request carries model, messages, system, tools and max_tokens
(the request's, else 8192) -- nothing else: no thinking, no temperature, no
cache markers.
@{bullet}Each HTTP call has a 60 s timeout. A 429 raises LLMRateLimitError
(LLMQuotaExhaustedError when the body says "quota" or "insufficient"), any
other refusal a plain error.
@{bullet}A batch that ended is judged by its request counts: any success is
completed; only cancelled requests make it cancelled, only expired ones
expired, anything else failed.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}Messages

@{bullet}All system messages are joined into the one top-level
@{tt}system@{utt} parameter, in order.
@{bullet}Developer notes -- the run speaking mid-conversation -- become user
turns wrapped in note tags: the Messages API has no role for them, and the
top-level system parameter would read as if the note had held from the first
turn. An entry declaring @{tt}developer_role@{utt} above user is logged and
lowered.
@{bullet}Tool results go back as @{tt}tool_result@{utt} blocks in a user turn.
Images a tool returned follow as a user message. An entry without a
@{tt}capabilities@{utt} block sends them; one with a block but without
@{tt}image_input: true@{utt} replaces them by a text note. Images in the
user's own messages are always sent.
@{bullet}Audio and video parts are dropped; text files become text blocks
starting @{tt}[File: <name>]@{utt}.
@{bullet}Arguments of an earlier tool call that are not valid JSON are
repaired for the replay, else sent as @{tt}{}@{utt}. The arguments of a new
call reach the agent exactly as the model sent them.

@{h2}Tools

Tool schemas are sent as @{tt}input_schema@{utt} with
@{tt}additionalProperties@{utt}, @{tt}examples@{utt}, @{tt}$schema@{utt} and
@{tt}$id@{utt} removed; every other constraint (minimum, pattern, enum,
oneOf ...) stays. A tool without parameters gets an empty object schema. The
model chooses freely whether to call a tool.

@{h2}Structured output

A JSON schema response format is sent as
@{tt}output_config.format@{utt}, beside the tools, when the entry declares
@{tt}capabilities.structured_output@{utt}. The Messages API has no
schema-less JSON mode, so a json_object format is refused before the
request. The schema goes out as written: the API refuses one it cannot
enforce with 400.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds LLM provider plugins by their
@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} (@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt}) and loads
@{tt}provider.py@{utt} when a model entry names @{tt}anthropic@{utt}. There
is no entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} and no allowlist line.

@{table}
Manifest key | Names
provides | anthropic
provides_batch | anthropic
@{body}

@{h2}Dependencies

The @{tt}anthropic@{utt} SDK, at least 0.77.0 (the first version that takes
@{tt}output_config@{utt}); it is imported when the first client is built, so
a missing package fails there with the plugin named. @{tt}httpx@{utt} comes
with it.

@{h2}TLS

With the app's @{tt}network.ssl_verify: false@{utt} the client uses its own
HTTP connection without certificate checks, for TLS-intercepting proxies. The
batch backend follows the app's setting too.

@{h2}Where things live

Nothing on disk. Changes to @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reach an agent when it builds
its client, i.e. after a restart.
@endnode
